WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.010
An agent checks the same regex again, and again, and again, until

00:00:04.010 --> 00:00:05.473
its token budget runs out.

00:00:05.473 --> 00:00:07.228
How would you even count that?

00:00:09.957 --> 00:00:10.961
Hi, I'm Dorrit!

00:00:10.961 --> 00:00:14.975
Nex-N2.6 is an open agent model that says it loops far less,

00:00:14.975 --> 00:00:17.652
and never calls a tool that isn't there.

00:00:17.652 --> 00:00:19.659
Let's see what those claims measure.

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:22.299
Chapter one.

00:00:22.299 --> 00:00:23.423
When reasoning loops.

00:00:28.467 --> 00:00:31.585
An agent gets a request, with the list of tools it may

00:00:31.585 --> 00:00:32.885
use, and starts to reason.

00:00:33.305 --> 00:00:34.416
It calls a tool.

00:00:34.416 --> 00:00:38.306
The harness runs it and hands back the output, and the model reasons again.

00:00:38.726 --> 00:00:41.746
A loop is when the reasoning folds back on itself.

00:00:41.746 --> 00:00:43.861
The same check, written again and again.

00:00:43.861 --> 00:00:46.881
The tool call never comes, and the episode times out.

00:00:48.883 --> 00:00:51.045
Take the looping trace from the article.

00:00:51.045 --> 00:00:52.280
Split it into words.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:53.206
Seventy-nine of them.

00:00:53.626 --> 00:00:55.467
Slide a window of six words along it.

00:00:56.733 --> 00:01:00.074
Any window that already appeared earlier is a repeat.

00:01:00.074 --> 00:01:01.930
Mark every word inside one.

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:03.415
Thirty-nine words get marked.

00:01:03.835 --> 00:01:06.520
So the repeated share is forty-nine point four percent.

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:10.601
Set the threshold at thirty percent, and this trace is flagged as a loop.

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:16.373
Every choice moves this number: the window length, the threshold, and whether

00:01:16.373 --> 00:01:18.132
a repeated tool error counts.

00:01:18.132 --> 00:01:20.243
Nex has not published its choices.

00:01:20.664 --> 00:01:23.223
Do that for every trace in a test set, and count

00:01:23.223 --> 00:01:24.154
how many get flagged.

00:01:24.574 --> 00:01:26.358
Divide by the number of traces.

00:01:26.358 --> 00:01:27.845
That is a repetition rate.

00:01:29.442 --> 00:01:31.155
Why do models loop at all?

00:01:31.155 --> 00:01:32.582
The research names four pushes.

00:01:33.002 --> 00:01:35.929
Decoding for the likeliest text walks straight into cycles.

00:01:36.349 --> 00:01:39.368
Each copy of a sentence makes the next copy more likely.

00:01:39.788 --> 00:01:43.889
Reinforcement learning drains the policy's entropy, so it has fewer ways out.

00:01:44.309 --> 00:01:48.207
And if long traces earn reward, looping is the cheapest way to be long.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:50.394
Chapter two.

00:01:50.394 --> 00:01:51.878
Where the drop came from.

00:01:57.050 --> 00:02:01.757
The launch post says repetition fell from sixteen percent to one point six.

00:02:01.757 --> 00:02:04.654
But N2.6 is built on a different base.

00:02:04.654 --> 00:02:07.550
The base alone was already at six percent.

00:02:09.425 --> 00:02:11.478
Nex's own chart prints that base column.

00:02:11.898 --> 00:02:15.363
Most of the fall from sixteen to six is already there when

00:02:15.363 --> 00:02:17.673
Nex runs its suite on the base model.

00:02:17.673 --> 00:02:20.849
Nex's own training takes it from six to one point six.

00:02:20.849 --> 00:02:22.004
That step is real.

00:02:23.842 --> 00:02:24.629
Chapter three.

00:02:24.629 --> 00:02:26.203
Selection is not success.

00:02:31.342 --> 00:02:33.908
Every request lists the tools the harness provides.

00:02:33.908 --> 00:02:36.153
The model has to use only those.

00:02:36.573 --> 00:02:41.193
Nex's metric checks one gate: is the tool name on the list?

00:02:41.193 --> 00:02:45.042
Across nine hundred and fifty-six contexts, N2.6 passed every time.

00:02:45.462 --> 00:02:47.443
It does not check the gates after it.

00:02:47.443 --> 00:02:49.424
Was it the right tool for this step?

00:02:49.424 --> 00:02:50.415
Were the arguments right?

00:02:50.835 --> 00:02:53.184
And a task is many calls in a row.

00:02:53.184 --> 00:02:56.578
A model that always called the shell would still score a hundred percent.

00:02:58.467 --> 00:02:59.221
Chapter four.

00:02:59.221 --> 00:03:00.353
Reading the scoreboard.

00:03:03.483 --> 00:03:08.020
Nex's table puts DeepSeek V four point one Flash at eighty-two

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:09.670
point seven, behind N2.6.

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:14.252
DeepSeek's own card says ninety point six, and eighty-two point seven sits

00:03:14.252 --> 00:03:15.640
under its older model.

00:03:17.508 --> 00:03:19.078
Compare a harder benchmark.

00:03:19.078 --> 00:03:23.787
Google DeepMind's Multimodal Knowledge Agent hands the agent real files, like these

00:03:23.787 --> 00:03:26.142
spectrum plots from a chip design.

00:03:26.562 --> 00:03:30.071
The agent must classify each marked spur and decide whether the

00:03:30.071 --> 00:03:31.347
chip can tape out.

00:03:31.347 --> 00:03:34.218
Its rubric grades the answer, not the tool name.

00:03:35.767 --> 00:03:39.691
A repetition rate is only as good as its window and its threshold.

00:03:39.691 --> 00:03:42.710
And a valid tool name is a gate, not success.

00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:44.521
Read both against the base model.

00:03:46.192 --> 00:03:46.824
To recap.

00:03:46.824 --> 00:03:49.354
A loop is repeated windows past a threshold.

00:03:49.354 --> 00:03:52.832
The base was already at six percent, and Nex took it

00:03:52.832 --> 00:03:54.097
to one point six.

00:03:54.097 --> 00:03:57.259
And a valid tool name is a gate, not success.

00:03:57.679 --> 00:03:59.745
Every source is in the full article.

00:03:59.745 --> 00:04:00.335
I'm Dorrit.

00:04:00.335 --> 00:04:00.630
Bye!
